      "content": "That's a good angle on the external factors, but the union's lawyers are going to dig deeper. They're going to look at the overlap between the high-fatigue telemetry and the actual clock-in/clock-out times. If they prove that these spikes happen consistently every single time a driver is on the new rotating shift, our 'weather and traffic' excuse falls apart and we're looking at a massive liability suit.\n\nCan you run a quick simulation to see if there's any way we can frame the schedule itself as a *preventative* measure against these spikes? Even if the data doesn't show it, I need a logical argument that makes it look like we're actually proactive about the fatigue issues we're seeing. If we can't win on the data, we need to win on the methodology.",
